  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Look for potential flooding rains into the morning along the\nsouthern Blue Ridge, then an isolated severe threat in the\nPiedmont late morning into the afternoon.\n\nRain continues to move south to north at a steady clip this\nmorning. Periods of heavier rain have been occurring but overall\nrates and duration have offset any flash flooding concerns.\nHowever, smaller rivers and creeks in the NC mountains are\nrising with a few in Watauga county in minor flood.\n\nSome rain gages in the mesonet network have had 3 to 5 inches in\nthe NC mountains into the VA Blue Ridge south of Floyd. \n\nHigh-res models tend to lean toward higher rainfall amounts\nshift to the east through the morning, but still enough in the\nflood watch area to keep it. Further east, dry antecedent\nconditions to preclude any flooding concerns outside some \nponding of water on roads, or any culverts blocked with leaves \ncausing some water overflowing ditches. The airmass will take on\na more convective look into the late morning with a band of \nshowers/few tstms moving up from central NC into the VA \nPiedmont. Will see how quickly the stable airmass gives way to \nsome destablization with higher dewpoints. Looking at convective\nmodels and neural network output along with pattern recognition\nfavors along/east of a Lynchburg to Yanceyville line to have \nany potential severe threats, with best chances further east. \nFurther west, confidence is low on storms and severe weather.\n\nThe gradient wind from the remnants of Isaias are also subsiding\nso may drop the wind advisory later this morning. Any winds over\n35 mph could still drop a weaker or dead tree so will wait til\ngusts overall drop under 35 mph before canceling. \n\nWe start drying out this afternoon from southwest to northeast\nthen may get some showers from the upper trough/Isaias remnant\nin the mountains this evening into the overnight.\n\nBy Monday, all remnants of Isaias will be well east of the \nregion with just some lingering wrap around upslope showers for\nthe western slopes through late Monday morning. Dry through most\nof the upcoming week with possibly the next chance of rain by\nthe second half of next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather into midweek with temps above\nnormal. \n\nBuilding ridge behind Isaias will bring temperatures back to\nabove normal through midweek. Another shortwave and front look\nto arrive anytime between Thursday and Saturday with model\ndifferences abounding. Ensembles/NBM favor some rain chances in\nthe mountains by the end of the week, with limited chances\nacross the Piedmont and temps dropping below normal next\nweekend.",
